I'm afraid you're reading that incorrectly.

dknown means you've seen the item (you know it's "a white gem" rather
than just "a gem")
oc_name_known means you've identified the class of item (so you see
diamonds as "a diamond")

So the condition is that thus code gets entered if you don't know
you're selling the shopkeeper a diamond (either because you haven't
seen it (or identified that one) or because you haven't yet identified
any diamonds to distinguish them from other white gems).

If the condition is met, the price the shopkeeper will offer per "gem"
is 3-8zm, selected in such a way that the same shopkeeper will always
offer the same price for the same gem type.

Note that this means:

* You don't have to identify the individual gem or know its BUC status
  to get full price, but you do have to know what kind of gem you are
  selling to get full price.
* Knowing that a gem is "valuable" (and naming it such) doesn't help;
  you still get the 3-8zm "he doesn't know what he's trying to sell"
  ripoff price.
* If you can figure out which gems are glass without identifying them,
  you can get a few zm off shopkeepers by selling them for
  3-8zm/piece. (IDed glass sells for 0)

There's a spoiler out there with all this information and details
about how to take advantage of the determinism in shopkeepers prices
for unknown gem types to effectively price-id gems.  If you're looking
to get money from shops by selling gems, this will help you determine
which gems to use an identify on to maximize profits.


